<h2 id="justified-heading">When Custom Is Justified</h2>
<h3>Non-Standard Product Geometry</h3>
<p>Products that are extremely long, heavy, irregularly shaped, or fragile may not work on standard conveyor widths and belt types. Custom conveyors can accommodate unusual product profiles, very flat items that need vacuum hold-down, cylindrical products that need V-shaped troughs, or heavy items that exceed standard roller load ratings. A <a href="/conveyor-functions/product-handling/spreader/">spreader conveyor</a> that handles a specific product width range, for example, may require custom lane guides and belt tensioning.</p>
<h3>Extreme Environments</h3>
<p>Standard conveyors are designed for indoor, ambient-temperature, non-corrosive environments. Applications that fall outside these parameters often require custom engineering: freezer environments (−40°F), high-temperature ovens (500°F+), <a href="/industries/pharmaceutical-conveyors/">cleanrooms</a> (ISO Class 5–7), explosive atmospheres (ATEX Zone 1/2), outdoor installations with UV exposure and weather sealing, or highly corrosive environments with chemical splash. Each of these constraints affects material selection, lubrication, sealing, motor rating, and belt material.</p>
<h3>Space Constraints</h3>
<p>Retrofit installations in existing facilities often face space constraints that catalog conveyors cannot accommodate: low ceiling heights requiring ultra-low-profile designs, narrow aisles requiring compact widths, specific elevation changes at precise locations, or curved paths around existing equipment. Custom conveyors can be designed to fit the available space exactly, whereas catalog conveyors require the space to be adapted to the equipment, which is often more expensive or impossible.</p>
<h3>Tight Integration Requirements</h3>
<p>When a conveyor must interface with specific upstream or downstream equipment, a robotic <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/case/robotic-case-packing/">case packer</a>, a <a href="/conveyor-functions/stacking-machines/">stacking machine</a>, or a labeling system, the conveyor's height, speed, <a href="/conveyor-functions/infeed-outfeed/">infeed/outfeed</a> position, and controls must match exactly. Custom conveyors designed around the integration points eliminate the transition brackets, height shims, and speed-matching workarounds that result from using standard models.</p>
<h3>Regulatory Compliance</h3>
<p>Some regulatory environments require design features that go beyond standard catalog options. USDA Dairy conveyors, for example, must meet 3-A Sanitary Standards with continuous-weld stainless steel construction, specific surface finish requirements (32 Ra or better), and self-draining geometry. ATEX-rated conveyors for explosive atmospheres need specific motor certifications, grounding provisions, and anti-static belt materials. These requirements typically push a project into custom territory.</p>
<h2 id="process-heading">The Custom Conveyor Design Process</h2>
<p>1</p>
<h3>Inquiry &amp; Requirements Definition</h3>
<p>The process starts with a detailed requirements document or request for quote (RFQ). The more complete your specification, the more accurate the initial quote. Include product data, throughput requirements, environment conditions, space constraints with facility drawings or photos, interface equipment details, and applicable standards. Most manufacturers have a standard questionnaire, ask for it.</p>
<p>2</p>
<h3>Concept Design &amp; Budgetary Quote</h3>
<p>The manufacturer develops a concept layout, typically a 2D drawing showing general arrangement, overall dimensions, and major components. This comes with a budgetary quote (typically ±15–20% accuracy). At this stage, you are evaluating whether the concept meets your needs and whether the budget is viable. Request concepts from 2–3 manufacturers to compare approaches.</p>
<p>3</p>
<h3>Detailed Engineering &amp; Firm Quote</h3>
<p>After selecting a manufacturer, detailed engineering begins: 3D CAD modeling, structural analysis (FEA for heavy loads), component selection (motors, drives, bearings, belting), and controls design. The manufacturer produces detailed drawings for your approval, general arrangement, assembly, electrical schematics, and a bill of materials. The firm quote (±5% accuracy) is issued after engineering is substantially complete.</p>
<p>4</p>
<h3>Fabrication &amp; Assembly</h3>
<p>Once drawings are approved and the purchase order is issued, fabrication begins. This includes cutting, welding, machining, surface finishing (painting, powder coating, or passivation for stainless), mechanical assembly, electrical wiring, and motor/drive installation. Fabrication is typically the longest phase, 6–12 weeks depending on complexity and material availability.</p>
<p>5</p>
<h3>Factory Acceptance Test (FAT)</h3>
<p>For critical or high-value custom conveyors, a factory acceptance test allows you to see the conveyor running at the manufacturer's facility before shipment. The FAT typically verifies speed range, load capacity, alignment, noise levels, and basic controls functionality. Bring your actual product (or representative samples) for testing. Any issues found during FAT are resolved before shipment, far less expensive than fixing problems after installation.</p>
<p>6</p>
<h3>Installation &amp; Commissioning</h3>
<p>Installation includes rigging, leveling, anchoring, utility connections (power, air, network), and integration with adjacent equipment. Commissioning covers startup, speed and alignment adjustments, controls integration testing, operator training, and documentation handoff. Clarify upfront who is responsible for installation, the manufacturer, your internal team, or a third-party contractor. Mixed responsibility (mechanical by one party, electrical by another) requires clear scope boundaries.</p>
<h2 id="spec-heading">What Manufacturers Need for a Custom Quote</h2>
<p>Incomplete specifications are the leading cause of inaccurate quotes, scope creep, and project delays. The checklist below covers the minimum information a manufacturer needs to provide a meaningful custom conveyor quote.</p>
<h3>Product Information</h3>
<ul>
<li>•Product dimensions (L × W × H), min, max, and typical</li>
<li>•Product weight, min, max, and typical per unit</li>
<li>•Product material (glass, plastic, cardboard, metal, food)</li>
<li>•Product temperature at point of conveyance</li>
<li>•Product fragility and surface sensitivity</li>
<li>•Product orientation requirements (label up, cap forward)</li>
</ul>
<h3>Performance Requirements</h3>
<ul>
<li>•Throughput rate (units/min, cases/hr, lbs/hr)</li>
<li>•Conveyor speed range (ft/min or m/min)</li>
<li>•Accumulation requirements (minutes of buffer needed)</li>
<li>•Indexing or continuous motion</li>
<li>•Operating schedule (shifts per day, days per week)</li>
<li>•Required uptime / availability percentage</li>
</ul>
<h3>Environment &amp; Facility</h3>
<ul>
<li>•Indoor / outdoor / both</li>
<li>•Temperature range (ambient and extremes)</li>
<li>•Washdown requirements (daily, per shift, CIP)</li>
<li>•Chemical exposure (acids, caustics, solvents)</li>
<li>•Available utilities (voltage, air pressure, network)</li>
<li>•Floor condition and loading capacity</li>
</ul>
<h3>Controls &amp; Integration</h3>
<ul>
<li>•Standalone or integrated with existing PLC/SCADA</li>
<li>•PLC platform preference (Allen-Bradley, Siemens, etc.)</li>
<li>•Network protocol (EtherNet/IP, PROFINET, Modbus)</li>
<li>•Upstream/downstream equipment and interface details</li>
<li>•Safety requirements (e-stops, guarding, light curtains)</li>
<li>•HMI requirements and operator interface needs</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="cost-heading">Managing Custom Conveyor Costs</h2>
<h3>Freeze the Scope Early</h3>
<p>Change orders are the primary driver of custom conveyor cost overruns. Every design change after engineering approval triggers re-engineering, potentially re-fabrication of components already in production, and schedule delays. Invest time in the requirements phase to get the specification right. If changes are unavoidable, make them before fabrication starts, changes during engineering cost a fraction of changes during fabrication.</p>
<h3>Use Standard Components Where Possible</h3>
<p>A custom conveyor does not mean every component must be custom. Specify standard motors, VFDs, bearings, <a href="/resources/conveyor-parts/">rollers</a>, and belting wherever possible. Custom components have longer lead times, higher costs, and create sole-source dependencies for spare parts. Tell the manufacturer you prefer standard catalog components and ask them to identify any proprietary items in their bill of materials.</p>
<h3>Consider Modified Standard Over Full Custom</h3>
<p>Many manufacturers can modify their standard platforms to meet 80–90% of custom requirements at a fraction of full custom cost. Non-standard widths, lengths, and heights are straightforward modifications. Adding or relocating drive positions, changing belt materials, and adding custom side guides are also common modifications. Ask manufacturers whether your requirements can be met with a modified standard product before assuming full custom is necessary.</p>
<h3>Order Spare Parts with the Conveyor</h3>
<p>Custom components manufactured as part of the original production run cost less than one-off replacement parts ordered later. Request a <a href="/resources/conveyor-maintenance/">recommended spare parts</a> list from the manufacturer and order critical spares, especially any proprietary items, with the initial purchase order. This is particularly important for custom belting, specialty rollers, and unique wear strips that may have long lead times when reordered.</p>
<h2 id="risk-heading">Managing Risk on Custom Projects</h2>
<h3>Manufacturer Selection</h3>
<p>Choose a <a href="/resources/conveyor-manufacturers/">manufacturer</a> with documented experience in your industry and conveyor type. Ask for references from similar custom projects, not just testimonials, but contact information for customers who operate similar equipment. A manufacturer's willingness to provide references speaks to their confidence in their work.</p>
<h3>Performance Guarantees</h3>
<p>Include measurable performance criteria in the purchase contract: throughput rate, speed range, noise level, power consumption, and uptime during the warranty period. Define how performance will be verified (what test protocol, what products, what conditions) and what happens if the conveyor does not meet the guaranteed performance. A manufacturer confident in their design will accept performance guarantees; one who hedges on guarantees may lack experience with your application.</p>
<h3>Factory Acceptance Testing</h3>
<p>For any custom conveyor with a purchase price above $$ range, a factory acceptance test (FAT) is strongly recommended. The cost of travel to the manufacturer's facility is negligible compared to the cost of discovering problems after installation. Bring actual product samples for testing and verify all performance criteria during the FAT. Document any deficiencies and require resolution before authorizing shipment.</p>
<h3>Documentation Requirements</h3>
<p>Specify documentation deliverables in the purchase order: as-built drawings (not just design drawings), electrical schematics, PLC program (if applicable), operating and maintenance manual, spare parts list with part numbers and suppliers, and material certifications (for food-contact or pharmaceutical applications). Documentation is often an afterthought, make it a contractual requirement with a delivery schedule.</p>
<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">How much more does a custom conveyor cost compared to a catalog model?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Custom conveyors typically cost 30–100% more than comparable catalog models, depending on the degree of customization. Minor modifications, non-standard width, length, or motor size, add 10–30%. Full custom designs with unique frame geometry, specialized materials, or integrated automation add 50–100% or more. The cost premium comes from engineering time, custom fabrication, longer lead times, and one-off testing. However, forcing a catalog conveyor into a non-standard application can cost more long-term through reduced throughput, higher maintenance, and workaround engineering.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">What is the typical lead time for a custom conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Custom conveyor lead times typically range from 8–20 weeks depending on complexity, materials, and the manufacturer's backlog. Simple modifications to standard platforms take 8–12 weeks. Fully custom designs with engineering approval, custom fabrication, and factory acceptance testing take 14–20 weeks or longer. Lead times increase for stainless steel construction, specialty coatings, imported components, or complex controls integration.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">When should I choose a custom conveyor over a standard model?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Custom conveyors are justified when standard models cannot meet your requirements for unusual product dimensions or weights, non-standard speeds or indexing patterns, tight space constraints, extreme environments (high temperature, corrosive chemicals, explosive atmospheres), regulatory requirements that standard models do not meet, integration with existing equipment at specific heights and positions, or throughput demands that exceed standard model capabilities. If a catalog conveyor meets 90%+ of your needs, a modified standard model is usually more cost-effective.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">What information do manufacturers need for a custom conveyor quote?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Manufacturers need: product description (dimensions, weight, material, temperature, fragility), throughput requirements (units per minute, pounds per hour), conveyor geometry (length, width, height, incline angle, curves), environment (indoor/outdoor, temperature range, washdown frequency, chemical exposure), material of construction preference (carbon steel, 304SS, 316SS, aluminum, HDPE), controls requirements (standalone, PLC integration, VFD, servo), interface points (infeed/outfeed heights, upstream/downstream equipment), applicable standards (FDA, USDA, ATEX, Class 100), and facility constraints (doorway dimensions, ceiling height, floor loading, power availability).</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-5">Can a custom conveyor be expanded or modified later?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-5" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>It depends on the original design. Custom conveyors built on modular frame systems (aluminum extrusion or welded steel with standard bolt patterns) can usually be extended, shortened, or reconfigured. Fully welded one-piece designs are harder to modify. When specifying a custom conveyor, ask the manufacturer about future expandability: can sections be added? Can the drive be upgraded? Can the speed range be increased? Building in modularity upfront costs slightly more but avoids replacing the entire conveyor if requirements change.</p>

</div>
</div>
